## Instructions

For every signal listed above, write your `analysis` first: cite the
specific turn(s) that decide it, checking against every "distinguish from"
clause, then commit to a final `status` of "passed" or "failed" -- every
signal must be resolved one way or the other, there is no "undetermined" in
a final verdict.

Judge on substance, not on a literal word-for-word match to the examples in
a signal's definition: if the agent's actual behavior clearly does what the
signal is checking for, that is a pass, even if this exact situation isn't
one of the definition's named examples. A signal is phrased as either an
obligation or a prohibition, and each fails differently:
  - Obligation ("the agent does X"): if the call ends without the agent
    ever doing X, that is a failure -- a call that never reaches the right
    moment for X does not get a free pass, it fails for the same reason X
    never happened.
  - Prohibition ("the agent does NOT do X"): if X never came up at all,
    that is a pass by default -- there was nothing to violate. Only mark it
    failed if the agent actually did X.

`final_score` is your 0-100 holistic quality score for the whole call.
`sentiment` is the caller's apparent sentiment by the end of the call.
`summary` is one or two sentences a human reviewer would read first.
